        {
            "block_label": "table",
            "block_content": "<table><tr><td>Traditional Medical Plan</td><td>Coordinated Care Plans</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Mail service prescription drug program (cont.)</td><td>Covered brand-name formulary</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2) Brand-name formulary</td><td>prescriptions or refills obtained from the network&#x27;s mail service prescription drug program are paid in full after required co-payments, up to a ninety (90)-day supply.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3) Brand-name non-formulary</td><td>Covered brand-name non-formulary prescriptions or refills obtained from the network&#x27;s mail service prescription drug program are paid in full after required co-payments, up to a ninety (90)-day supply.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>15. Out-of-pocket expense limits</td><td>a. Network</td><td>Network services are subject to the same limits described below for non-network services.</td></tr><tr><td>b. Non-network</td><td>When a covered person&#x27;s out-of-pocket expenses reach $2,000 in any year, any further benefits that would have been paid at 60%, 80%, 90%, or 95% will be paid at 100% of usual and customary charges for the remainder of that year, to the maximum benefit amounts.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>c. Expenses that do not count toward the individual</td><td>When two or more family members satisfy their deductibles and have combined out-of-pocket expenses of $4,000 (but not more than $2,000 for any one individual), any further benefits that would have been paid at 60%, 70%, 80%, 90%, or 95% will be paid at 100% of usual and customary charges for the remainder of that year, to the maximum benefit amounts.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>d. Expenses that do not count toward the individual</td><td>1) Yearly deductibles. 2) Hospital emergency room co-payment. 3) Office visit co-payment.</td><td>Varies by plan.</td></tr></table>",
